# Week B (Mon 2 to Sun 8 Nov 2026) voiceovers. OWASP API Security Top 10 (2023) + fundamentals.
# Numbers are spelled out so the transcript check and caption anchors stay clean.
S = {
# MON 2
"02-0700": "Send any token to some APIs, and the door opens. Expired, edited, or signed by someone else. Broken authentication is number two on the OWASP API list, and it often comes down to one missing check. The server reads the token, but never verifies it. Check four things on every request. Is the signature valid? Has it expired? Was it issued by you, for this API? Has it been revoked? Then test it. Send an expired token and an edited one, and expect both to be rejected.",
"02-0900": "If you sell software to companies in the European Union, NIS two is already reaching your inbox. It is an EU law for essential and important companies, like banks, energy, health and digital providers. They must manage security risk in their supply chain. That means you. Expect security questionnaires, contract clauses on incident reporting, and requests for evidence. Get ready now. A named security owner. An incident process that can tell customers within hours. And proof that you patch and control access.",
"02-1300": "Some tokens sign themselves. Change the header of a JWT to algorithm none, remove the signature, and a badly configured library accepts it. Three common JWT mistakes. One. Accepting whatever algorithm the token asks for. Pin the algorithm on the server. Two. Weak signing secrets that can be cracked offline. Use a long random key, or asymmetric keys. Three. Tokens that never expire. Keep access tokens short-lived, and refresh them. A JWT is only as strong as the code that checks it.",
"02-1500": "Developers make some of the best application security engineers, because they already know how software is built. The honest path looks like this. First, fix security bugs in code you already own. Second, learn the common attacks by breaking practice apps, never production. Third, join code reviews and ask what an attacker would do. Fourth, write up what you learn, in public. Within a year you can show real fixes, real reviews and real write-ups. That is what hiring teams want to see.",
"02-1700": "A six digit code has one million possible values. Without a rate limit, a script can try them all before the code expires. Login, one-time codes and password reset need limits the most. Limit attempts per account, not just per IP address, because attackers rotate addresses. Slow down or lock after a few failures. Expire a code after a few wrong tries. And alert when one account sees a burst of failures. Guessing only works when guessing is free.",
"02-1900": "Where does your web app keep its tokens? In a cookie, or in local storage? Cookies marked HTTP only can't be read by JavaScript. Local storage can be read by any script on the page. Both have trade-offs. Which do you use, and why?",
# TUE 3
"03-0700": "The field you hid in the app is still in the API response. Hiding a field in the interface is not access control. Number three on the OWASP API list is broken object property level authorization. It covers two problems. Reading properties you should not see, like another user's email or an internal score. And changing properties you should not touch, like a role or a price. The fix. Decide, for every endpoint, which fields each role can read and write, and enforce it on the server.",
"03-0900": "A customer in the US or Europe just asked for your SOC two report. Here is what that means. SOC two is an independent audit of your security controls, against criteria set by the American accounting body, the AICPA. A type one report checks that your controls are designed well, on one date. A type two report checks that they actually worked, over several months. Most customers want type two. Start with what auditors test first. Access reviews, change management, monitoring and incident response.",
"03-1300": "Your app shows a name and a photo. Your API sends the whole user record. Email, phone number, date of birth, internal flags. Anyone who opens the network tab can read it. This is excessive data exposure. It happens when an endpoint returns the database object and leaves the app to filter it. Filter on the server instead. Build each response with only the fields that screen needs. Then open your busiest endpoints in a proxy, and count the fields the app never uses.",
"03-1500": "Dropped into a codebase you have never seen, with a security review due Friday? Don't read it top to bottom. Start where the risk is. First, authentication. How a user proves who they are. Second, authorization. Where the code checks what they are allowed to do. Third, every place money or personal data is written. Fourth, anything that takes input from outside, like uploads, webhooks and links. Most serious bugs live in those four places. The rest can wait.",
"03-1700": "Some attacks never steal data. They just send you the bill. Number four on the OWASP API list is unrestricted resource consumption. An endpoint that sends text messages, builds reports or calls a paid AI model can be triggered thousands of times, and every call costs you money. Put limits on everything that costs. Requests per user, page sizes, upload sizes, and spending caps with your providers. And alert on unusual spend, because the first sign is often the invoice.",
"03-1900": "Open your app's network traffic and look at one API response. How many fields does it return that the screen never shows? Most teams are surprised by the answer. Have you checked yours?",
# WED 4
"04-0700": "A normal user calls an admin endpoint, and it works. Number five on the OWASP API list is broken function level authorization. The admin page is hidden in the app, but the API behind it never checks the role. Attackers find these endpoints by guessing names, reading the app's code, or watching traffic. The fix. Every endpoint checks the caller's role on the server, and new endpoints deny by default. Then test each admin endpoint with a normal user's token.",
"04-0900": "If you sell software or connected products in the EU, security is becoming a legal product requirement. That is the Cyber Resilience Act. Since September this year, makers must report actively exploited vulnerabilities within twenty four hours. From December twenty twenty seven, products must be secure by default, receive security updates for their support period, and offer a way to report vulnerabilities. For product teams, that means a vulnerability process, a list of components, and an update plan. Starting now.",
"04-1300": "Hiding the delete button from normal users is good design. It is not security. Anyone can send the request that button would have sent. Role checks must live on the server, on every action. Keep them in one place, like middleware or a policy layer, instead of scattered through the code. Deny by default, so a new endpoint stays closed until someone opens it. And test with two accounts, an admin and a normal user, sending the same requests.",
"04-1500": "Product managers don't ignore security. They weigh it against everything else on the roadmap. So speak their language. Describe risk as impact on customers, revenue or launch dates, not as a severity score. Offer options, not one answer. Fix now, fix after launch with a guardrail, or accept the risk. Size the work honestly. And bring good news too. Security engineers who help product make decisions get invited to planning, not just the post mortem.",
"04-1700": "Your new sneaker drop sold out in eight seconds. Not to customers. To bots. Number six on the OWASP API list is unrestricted access to sensitive business flows. There is no bug in the code, just a valuable flow with no limits. Buying stock, booking slots, creating accounts, posting reviews. Find the flows that hurt when automated. Then add friction where it matters. Device and behaviour signals, limits per account, queues for big drops, and checks before money or stock leaves.",
"04-1900": "Who owns API security in your team? The backend developers, a security team, the platform team, or nobody in particular? Ownership decides what gets tested. Who is it for you?",
# THU 5
"05-0700": "Your app fetches a profile picture from a link the user provides. What if that link points inside your network? Number seven on the OWASP API list is server-side request forgery. The attacker makes your server send requests to places they can't reach. Internal admin panels, databases, or cloud metadata. The fix. Allow-list the domains you fetch from, block private and internal addresses, check again after every redirect, and run the fetching code with as little network access as possible.",
"05-0900": "One request to the right internal address, and an attacker can walk away with your cloud keys. Cloud servers have a metadata service that hands out temporary credentials. If your app has a server-side request forgery bug, an attacker can make it ask for them. One of the best known cloud breaches started exactly this way. On AWS, require IMDS version two, which needs a session token a simple forged request can't get. Limit the role on each server. And block metadata addresses in any code that fetches links.",
"05-1300": "A user uploads a profile picture. It isn't a picture. It's a script, a huge file, or an image with a hidden payload. File upload is one of the riskiest features you can ship. Check the real file type, not the name or the type the browser claims. Set size limits. Rename files and keep them in separate storage, away from your application. Serve them from a different domain. And never let an uploaded file run as code.",
"05-1500": "A pentest only finds what its scope lets it find. Scope it badly, and you pay for a clean report on the wrong thing. Start with what would hurt most. Money movement, customer data, admin access. Put those flows in scope, with test accounts for every role. Include the APIs and mobile apps, not just the website. Agree on what is off limits and how to reach you in an emergency. And book the retest before the test starts.",
"05-1700": "One error message told the attacker your framework, your database and the line that failed. Number eight on the OWASP API list is security misconfiguration. Stack traces in production. Cross-origin settings that trust any website. Debug endpoints left on. Missing security headers. Defaults nobody reviewed. The fix. Generic errors for users, detailed logs for you, strict cross-origin rules, and a hardened configuration that is checked automatically on every deploy.",
"05-1900": "Trigger an error in your API on purpose. What does the response reveal? A stack trace, a database name, a framework version? Or just a clean message? Try it today. What did you find?",
# FRI 6
"06-0700": "You launched version three of your API last year. Version one is still online, still connected to production data, and still missing every fix since. Number nine on the OWASP API list is improper inventory management. Old versions, beta endpoints and forgotten test hosts become the easiest way in. Keep a living list of every API host and version, with an owner and a retirement date. Retire old versions on schedule. And scan for hosts that aren't on the list.",
"06-0900": "If your company is going for ISO twenty seven thousand and one, the auditor will talk to engineers, not just managers. Expect questions like these. Who approved this production change, and where is the record? When were access rights last reviewed? How fast are critical vulnerabilities fixed? When was a backup last restored? Every answer needs evidence. Tickets, logs and dates. Build that evidence into your normal workflow, and audits stop being a scramble.",
"06-1300": "The breach didn't come from production. It came from staging, which held a copy of real customer data and half the security. Staging environments often have weaker access controls, shared passwords, debug mode and no monitoring. Fill them with production data, and they become the easiest target in the company. Use synthetic or masked data instead. If real data is truly needed, protect staging like production. And delete old copies on a schedule.",
"06-1500": "Certifications or skills. Which gets you hired in security? Honestly, both, for different reasons. Certifications get you past filters, especially at large companies and in regulated industries. Skills get you through the interview, and through the job. The strongest profile pairs one relevant certification with visible proof. Write-ups, tools you've built, findings you've reported. Hiring managers remember the candidate who walked them through a real bug, not the longest list of acronyms.",
"06-1700": "You validate everything your users send. What about everything your partners' APIs send back? Number ten on the OWASP API list is unsafe consumption of APIs. Teams treat third-party responses as trusted, and pass them straight into databases, pages or payments. If the partner is compromised, so are you. Validate third-party data like user input. Use encrypted connections, follow redirects carefully, set timeouts, and limit what each integration can trigger.",
"06-1900": "Ten risks on the OWASP API list this week. Which one do you see most in real systems? Broken object authorization, broken authentication, misconfiguration, or something else? Tell me which one, and where.",
# SAT 7
"07-0700": "One GraphQL query can ask for every record in your database, if you let it. Introspection hands attackers a map of your whole schema. Batching lets them send thousands of password guesses in a single request, slipping past rate limits. And deeply nested queries can exhaust your server. Turn introspection off in production. Limit query depth and complexity. Count each operation in a batch against your rate limits. And check authorization on every field, not just the top-level query.",
"07-0900": "Your API changes every day. A yearly pentest checks it once. Automate the basics on every merge. First, scan dependencies and secrets. Second, check your API specification for endpoints with no authentication. Third, run authorization tests. The same request as two different users, expecting one to fail. Fourth, scan the running app in a test environment. Fail the build only on serious issues, so developers trust it. Then save human testers for logic and business flows.",
"07-1300": "CORS doesn't protect your API. It decides which websites a browser lets read your API's responses. Scripts, servers and attack tools ignore it completely. So it is not access control. But a bad setting can still hurt you. Reflecting any origin while allowing credentials lets a malicious site read a logged-in user's data. Allow only your own domains, and never a wildcard with credentials. And protect the API itself with real authentication and authorization.",
"07-1500": "Your CEO doesn't need to know what an injection is. They need to know what could happen, how likely it is, and what it costs to fix. Use one sentence for impact. An attacker could download every customer's address. Say how hard it is to exploit, in plain words. Give the fix, its cost and its timeline. And compare it to a risk they already understand. Clear explanations get budgets. Jargon gets postponed.",
"07-1700": "You deleted the file with the API key. The key is still in your Git history, and anyone with the repository can find it. Once a secret is committed, treat it as leaked. Rotate it first, then clean the history if you need to. Then prevent the next one. Scan for secrets before every commit and in your pipeline. Keep secrets in a secrets manager, never in code. And check old repositories, forks and public mirrors too.",
"07-1900": "What's the last secret you found somewhere it shouldn't be? A key in a repository, a password in a chat, a token in a log? No names. Where was it, and how long had it been there?",
# SUN 8
"08-0700": "API key or OAuth token? They solve different problems. An API key identifies an application. It is simple, long lived, and often has broad access. Good for server to server calls you control. An OAuth token represents a user's permission, with a limited scope and a short life. Use it when an app acts on behalf of a user. Whichever you use, scope it, rotate it, and never ship a secret key inside a mobile app.",
"08-0900": "The attacker didn't break into your company. They broke into a vendor that had admin access to it. Support tools, payroll systems, analytics scripts, outsourced developers. Each one can be a way in. List every vendor with access to your systems or data. Give each one the least access it needs, with separate accounts and multi-factor authentication. Review that access every quarter. And make sure contracts require them to tell you quickly when they are breached.",
"08-1300": "When something goes wrong, the first question is, what happened? Without the right logs, nobody can answer. If you log nothing else, log these three. Authentication. Logins, failures, password resets and new devices. Admin actions. Role changes, exports and configuration changes. Money. Every payment, refund and payout, with who and when. Send logs somewhere attackers can't delete them. And never log passwords, tokens or full card numbers.",
"08-1500": "One security engineer can't review every pull request in a company of two hundred developers. A security champions programme helps. One interested developer per team, with a few hours a month for security. Give them training, a direct line to the security team, and real decisions to make. They catch issues in design, not after release, and they spread good habits faster than any policy. Recognise their work, and make it count toward promotion.",
"08-1700": "One small package, used by thousands of apps. When it is compromised, so is every app that installs it. Most of what you ship is other people's code. Keep a list of what you use, with a software bill of materials. Pin versions and commit lock files. Scan for known vulnerabilities on every build. Be careful with new and little-known packages. And when a critical issue drops, know within an hour which of your apps are affected.",
"08-1900": "How many dependencies does your main app have, including the ones your dependencies pull in? Ten? Hundreds? Thousands? Most teams guess low. Check, and tell me the real number.",
}
